  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty showers and thunderstorms continue this \nafternoon and will be possible again tomorrow afternoon.\n\nSpotty showers and thunderstorms have popped up this afternoon in SE \nMA and along the south shore as a surface front pushes through the \nregion. Interactions with the sea breeze boundary have also\nramped these up, some turning severe. Showers and storms have \nalso popped up just NW of MA, moving SE which may end up \nimpacting interior MA. Winds have already shifted more NW in \nareas behind the front. These showers and storms will continue \nto pop up through the rest of the afternoon but will diminish \nheading into tonight. A thunderstorm risk exists for tomorrow as\nwell, though the risk of them turning severe is low and limited\nbased on the latest CAMs.\n\nFollowing the front, drier air will be ushered into the region. This \nwill bring PWATs closer to an inch, diminishing further following \nFriday's cold front. HREF guidance has values falling to as low as \naround 0.5\". The chance for isolated showers and storms returns in \nthe afternoon tomorrow, though  Temperatures will remain seasonable \nwith highs in the 80s. Lows tonight will dip into the mid 50s, then \ncooler tomorrow night with a greater possibility for radiational \ncooling. Urban areas will remain slightly warmer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry and pleasant weekend in store for the region.\n\nSprawling continental high pressure builds S from Canada this \nweekend leading to mild and pleasant days and cool nights. \nAirmass will be quite dry with column-average PWATs falling to \n0.4-0.5 inches. This fall-like airmass will be a welcome break \nfrom our recent stretch of humidity where dew point temperatures\nhave been in the upper 60s to low 70s for the past several \nweeks. It will be a mild stretch with the early August sun \nbringing temperatures near or slightly below seasonable levels, \nranging from the upper 70s to lower 80s. Saturday night into \nSunday morning, clear skies and light winds provide favorable \nconditions for effective radiational cooling. Lows Saturday \nnight range from the low to mid 50s for the majority of the CWA \nand near 60 closer to urban centers. Some guidance even suggests\nlow in the upper 40s across portions of Northern Massachusetts.\n\nHigh pressure moves offshore early Sunday, allowing for an \nincreasing SWrly return flow. The SW winds bring the return of 60s \ndewpoints though temperatures remain comfortable with highs in the \nupper 70s. Clouds will be on the increase as a shortwave and \nassociated frontal system approach from the west later Sunday \nafternoon into the evening. Guidance shows a low level jet \ndeveloping and bringing elevated PWATs over the region after dark \nSunday. More details about this rainfall threat in the next key \nmessage. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Rain, possibly heavy at times, Sunday night into \nMonday morning. \n\nThe next opportunity for rainfall comes during the second half of \nSunday into Monday. However, there remains uncertainty regarding \nrainfall amounts, as the best forcing is associated with the surface \nlow and track variability makes it difficult to pin down where the \ngreatest rainfall will occur. This is worth monitoring, as global \nguidance is in agreement that PWATs may exceed 2 inches, indicating \nthe potential for heavy rainfall. This is also supported by a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. Global \nensembles highlight areas north of I-90 as having the greatest \nlikelihood for measurable rainfall, with a 40-60 percent probability \nof at least a half inch of rain between Sunday evening and Monday \nevening. That said, if rainfall becomes more convective, localized \ntotals could be higher. One positive is that this system appears \nprogressive, with the cold front clearing east of New England on \nMonday. Drier air then advects in from the northwest, leading to \nanother dry stretch from the latter half of Monday through Tuesday \nand Wednesday as surface high pressure builds back into the region. \nDuring this time, temperatures are expected to remain seasonable, \nwith highs in the lower to middle 80s and tolerable humidity as dew \npoints remain in the lower 60s.",
